Because there[’]s often so many factors[ that] can drive business, it[ can] be overwhelming to know[ where] to start digging for answers. I find it helpful to generate a[ list] of mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive ([M]ECE) probable hypotheses to easily identify the top contenders and eliminate[ those that] are less likely. This planning and forethought always saves me time because it provides a targeted list of analyses to consider, and every analysis assists in either proving or disproving the most likely hypothetical causes. By setting myself up for success on the front end, I find more time to generate insights and implications on the back end.
It always helps to understand the whole picture. It[’]s not enough to say that a[ brand] is growing 10%. I always make data points relevant to my audience by benchmarking it back to something. Is that 10[%] accelerated or decelerated from the prior year?[ Is that 10%] higher or lower than the category?[ Is that] 10[%] better or worse than projected estimates? The easiest way to make data mean something is by making a comparative[ �]�than” statement.
